    "doi": "https://doi.org/10.5194/acp-11-8777-2011",
    "abstract": "Abstract. For oceans to be a significant source of primary organic aerosol (POA), sea spray aerosol (SSA) must be highly enriched with organics relative to the bulk seawater. We propose that organic enrichment at the air-sea interface, chemical composition of seawater, and the aerosol size are three main parameters controlling the organic mass fraction of sea spray aerosol (OMSSA). To test this hypothesis, we developed a new marine POA emission function based on a conceptual relationship between the organic enrichment at the air-sea interface and surface wind speed. The resulting parameterization is explored using aerosol chemical composition and surface wind speed from Atlantic and Pacific coastal stations, and satellite-derived ocean concentrations of chlorophyll-a, dissolved organic carbon, and particulate organic carbon. Of all the parameters examined, a multi-variable logistic regression revealed that the combination of 10 m wind speed and surface chlorophyll-a concentration ([Chl-a]) are the most consistent predictors of OMSSA. This relationship, combined with the published aerosol size dependence of OMSSA, resulted in a new parameterization for the organic mass fraction of SSA. Global emissions of marine POA are investigated here by applying this newly-developed relationship to existing sea spray emission functions, satellite-derived [Chl-a], and modeled 10 m winds. Analysis of model simulations shows that global annual submicron marine organic emission associated with sea spray is estimated to be from 2.8 to 5.6 Tg C yr\u22121. This study provides additional evidence that marine primary organic aerosols are a globally significant source of organics in the atmosphere.",
    "field": "Atmospheric aerosol chemistry",
    "problem": "Existing parameterizations for the organic fraction of sea spray aerosol in climate models inadequately account for the combined effects of wind speed, ocean biology, and aerosol size, leading to significant uncertainties in predicting aerosol loads and their subsequent impact on climate.",
    "method": "The authors propose a new parameterization for the organic mass fraction of sea spray aerosol using a multi-variable logistic regression that combines the effects of 10 m wind speed, surface chlorophyll-a concentration, and published aerosol size-dependence relationships.",
    "task": "The task is to develop a new, wind speed- and size-dependent parameterization for the organic mass fraction of sea spray aerosol using oceanic chlorophyll-a concentration and wind speed to estimate global emissions of marine primary organic aerosol.",
